1
00:00:00,511 --> 00:00:02,772
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm Guru Nikaya 5.175.

2
00:00:02,872 --> 00:00:05,552
[SPEAKER_00]: This is called the outcast.

3
00:00:06,713 --> 00:00:14,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Endowed with these five qualities, a lay follower, upāsaka, is an outcast of a lay follower, a stain of a lay follower.

4
00:00:15,455 --> 00:00:19,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Number one, he does not have faith in the Buddha and the Saṅgha.

5
00:00:20,316 --> 00:00:21,256
[SPEAKER_00]: He is unvirtuous.

6
00:00:22,477 --> 00:00:27,098
[SPEAKER_00]: He is superstitious and believes in auspicious signs and not in action.

7
00:00:28,843 --> 00:00:30,725
[SPEAKER_00]: He seeks... That's a big one.

8
00:00:30,745 --> 00:00:32,286
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the one I want to speak about as well.

9
00:00:32,907 --> 00:00:39,873
[SPEAKER_00]: He seeks outside the saṅgha for a person worthy of offerings, and he first does notorious deeds there.

10
00:00:41,114 --> 00:00:46,559
[SPEAKER_00]: So, possessing these five qualities, a lay follower is a stain of a lay follower.

11
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:54,666
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in particular, number three, he is superstitious, and he believes in auspicious signs, not in action, karma.

12
00:00:56,960 --> 00:00:59,122
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's kind of... Well, most people, really.

13
00:00:59,582 --> 00:01:00,203
[SPEAKER_01]: To a degree.

14
00:01:00,263 --> 00:01:01,484
[SPEAKER_01]: To different degrees, yes.

15
00:01:03,165 --> 00:01:10,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Because how many Buddhists believe in chanting?

16
00:01:10,391 --> 00:01:25,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's not these kind of rituals and ritual practices, even if it's your own virtue, if you don't understand the root of your action or restraint in regard to your virtue,

17
00:01:26,359 --> 00:01:39,907
[SPEAKER_01]: You are implying, you're believing, implicitly, that abstaining from certain behavior and cultivating certain other behavior will magically purify your mind, will result in the purification of your mind.

18
00:01:39,967 --> 00:01:44,410
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see how, you don't understand, and you certainly have no direct input.

19
00:01:45,030 --> 00:01:46,491
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already superstitious view.

20
00:01:46,611 --> 00:01:48,192
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already Sīlabhāta Paramās.

21
00:01:50,187 --> 00:02:09,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if there are no external, as I said, crude ritualistic behaviors and worships and beliefs and so on, just not understanding the roots of your actions, your own precepts, your own virtue, will not be... will be a fetter, will be sīlabhātu paramāsa.

22
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:16,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, obviously, you abandon sīlabhātu paramāsa not by abandoning virtue, but by abandoning your view in regard to it.

23
00:02:17,673 --> 00:02:22,756
[SPEAKER_01]: View being, I expect this set of norms and behaviors to purify the mind for me.

24
00:02:27,359 --> 00:02:32,523
[SPEAKER_01]: So, understanding action is what most people don't, basically.

25
00:02:33,684 --> 00:02:34,504
[SPEAKER_01]: That's pretty much.

26
00:02:35,685 --> 00:02:39,367
[SPEAKER_01]: And even, even all the, like, the religious teachers back in the day, they didn't.

27
00:02:39,388 --> 00:02:40,088
[SPEAKER_01]: They overlooked it.

28
00:02:41,609 --> 00:02:46,032
[SPEAKER_01]: And they sought enlightenment here and there, and in these practices, and in extreme commitment.

29
00:02:47,398 --> 00:02:52,102
[SPEAKER_01]: ...to the practice and endurance and everything else, and they missed what was right in front of their noses.

30
00:02:53,583 --> 00:02:56,124
[SPEAKER_01]: The root of karma, it's right here, right now.

31
00:02:56,605 --> 00:02:57,746
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your own intentions.

32
00:02:59,707 --> 00:03:11,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Discerning that as the basis for practice, and then aligning your behavior by body, speech and mind, with that discernment, that's the way out.

33
00:03:16,915 --> 00:03:30,783
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by doing acts which result in craving, well, that are from craving that result in suffering... By abstaining from those acts, so that you can understand the root of action.

34
00:03:32,063 --> 00:03:40,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Because certain acts inevitably carry greed, aversion, delusion and cause greed, aversion, delusion to increase.

35
00:03:42,929 --> 00:03:45,611
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once the Buddha recognized that, he abstained from those acts.

36
00:03:47,188 --> 00:03:49,830
[SPEAKER_01]: By body speech and mentally, he was not entertaining them.

37
00:03:51,271 --> 00:03:52,332
[SPEAKER_01]: He was not indulging them.

38
00:03:54,513 --> 00:03:56,955
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when the mind dried up from that, he understood.

39
00:03:58,236 --> 00:04:03,500
[SPEAKER_01]: My behavior is as pure as it can be now, but it's still bound with action.

40
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:04,801
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still bound with intention.

41
00:04:04,881 --> 00:04:06,803
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still bound with him doing that.

42
00:04:07,783 --> 00:04:09,284
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, he started looking further.

43
00:04:10,065 --> 00:04:12,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Within the same practice, obviously, not elsewhere.

44
00:04:17,263 --> 00:04:18,223
[SPEAKER_01]: And what did he discover?

45
00:04:18,243 --> 00:04:31,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he discovered that the root of the wholesome actions is fundamentally subjected to anicca and dukkha, because of its impermanence, because of its unknown ability.

46
00:04:33,209 --> 00:04:42,932
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the mind now, having been dried up from greed, aversion, delusion, left with wholesome intentions, wholesome actions, understood that doors cannot be owned, even if you want to.

47
00:04:47,803 --> 00:04:51,205
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sabhe, saṅkhara, anicca, sabhe, saṅkhara, dukkha, for that very reason.

48
00:04:51,845 --> 00:04:54,767
[SPEAKER_01]: And that then results in the experience of sabhe, dhamma, anatta.

49
00:04:56,288 --> 00:05:08,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding that these acts, even the previous acts, that you have abandoned, because they were clouding your perspective, narrowing perspective even more, they do all share the same nature, good and bad.

50
00:05:09,936 --> 00:05:16,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, unownable, subject to destruction, impermanent, cannot be yours.

51
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:18,721
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was the way out.

52
00:05:21,625 --> 00:05:23,188
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like the most direct way.

53
00:05:23,909 --> 00:05:30,558
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no... there is no... there is no middleman, so to speak.

54
00:05:32,607 --> 00:05:37,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Between your practice, if done rightly, and insight and results of it.

55
00:05:37,591 --> 00:05:46,817
[SPEAKER_01]: That even if, say, Sotāpanna, who is still not an Arahant, he still has work to do, when he attends to things rightly, with yoniso, manasikāra, and his right view, he sees nibbāna.

56
00:05:46,857 --> 00:05:48,218
[SPEAKER_01]: He sees exactly what nibbāna is.

57
00:05:48,739 --> 00:05:50,980
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows exactly how to get there.

58
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:59,126
[SPEAKER_01]: But, of course, now he has to keep walking there, and protecting that, and cultivating that behavior sufficiently until it's fully developed.

59
00:06:00,484 --> 00:06:05,906
[SPEAKER_01]: But he has no doubt as to where the problem is and where the solution is.

60
00:06:05,946 --> 00:06:08,547
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why he's beyond Sīla Bhārata Paramāsa.

61
00:06:08,567 --> 00:06:09,087
[SPEAKER_01]: He's free.

62
00:06:09,327 --> 00:06:11,808
[SPEAKER_01]: He cannot be superstitious, even if he wants to.

63
00:06:12,668 --> 00:06:17,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that already requires an external agent of chance, circumstance, magic, whatever.

64
00:06:20,170 --> 00:06:24,612
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not where the problem was, which is not where the solution can come from either.

65
00:06:31,318 --> 00:06:34,400
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are responsible for your suffering.

66
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:38,642
[SPEAKER_00]: There's not something else that's making you suffer.

67
00:06:40,923 --> 00:06:47,226
[SPEAKER_00]: That's now that you have to appeal to, to grant you freedom from it.

68
00:06:47,826 --> 00:06:48,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

69
00:06:48,046 --> 00:06:49,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you could look at it like that.

70
00:06:49,527 --> 00:06:56,390
[SPEAKER_01]: You could look at it even in, like, say... even if... even in a subtler manner.

71
00:06:56,530 --> 00:07:00,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, you recognize that your mind is not in your control.

72
00:07:01,842 --> 00:07:10,668
[SPEAKER_01]: That your mind is a wild animal that's every now and then subjecting you to suffering through its own like thoughts and and refusal to listen to you and so on.

73
00:07:11,949 --> 00:07:12,910
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you go about it?

74
00:07:13,930 --> 00:07:20,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you roll up your sleeve, take the whip, leash that animal and you start the training?

75
00:07:21,475 --> 00:07:29,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Or do you go and bow to the shrine and chant and do all these practices, hoping that that will calm the mind, which then in return won't bother you?

76
00:07:34,707 --> 00:07:38,671
[SPEAKER_01]: It boils down to that.

77
00:07:40,553 --> 00:07:47,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Or go on a retreat, protect the environment, enter a peaceful state and then hope it will last.

78
00:07:47,119 --> 00:07:52,784
[SPEAKER_01]: Hopefully, this mind will not turn against me when I get out of the retreat and when life starts hitting me again.

79
00:07:53,648 --> 00:08:04,351
[SPEAKER_01]: But, either way, there is no direct communication between you and your mind and there's certainly no understanding as to... direct understanding as to what calms this mind and what agitates it.

80
00:08:04,511 --> 00:08:07,852
[SPEAKER_01]: There is only the approximations, hope and wishful thinking.

81
00:08:13,774 --> 00:08:17,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you say you're not going to be able to see, eh...

82
00:08:19,226 --> 00:08:24,847
[SPEAKER_00]: sabhe, dhamma, anatta, all things are not my own, myself.

83
00:08:26,648 --> 00:08:28,368
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't cultivate the wholesome...

84
00:08:28,608 --> 00:08:34,869
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not withdrawn thoroughly from the unwholesome, yeah, unwholesome intentions and actions by body, speech and mind.

85
00:08:35,310 --> 00:08:38,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is that, you know, that sutta and some people get confused about it.

86
00:08:38,970 --> 00:08:41,411
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, I am the owner of my actions.

87
00:08:41,931 --> 00:08:42,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

88
00:08:43,277 --> 00:08:44,557
[SPEAKER_00]: So, some people say with...

89
00:08:44,758 --> 00:08:47,920
[SPEAKER_00]: I am not the owner of the body, the feelings, the perceptions, the intentions.

90
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:49,281
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're an arhant, you're not.

91
00:08:49,781 --> 00:08:54,245
[SPEAKER_00]: But I am the owner of my... Yeah, because you start as a non-arhant.

92
00:08:54,865 --> 00:09:00,350
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to become an arhant is to first take responsibility and ownership of that which you assume the ownership of.

93
00:09:01,410 --> 00:09:03,332
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just be a putujana

94
00:09:05,477 --> 00:09:16,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Still very much assuming ownership of things, and then just learn the information that arahant is not the owner of his actions because he's an arahant and he's freed from his aggregates and so on, and then just start applying that information to you.

95
00:09:19,749 --> 00:09:28,897
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like applying theory to the practice that you haven't developed, but saying that it applies to you because... well, because of wishful thinking, because of an excuse.

96
00:09:35,097 --> 00:09:39,719
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like saying, basically, if you apply yourself and train yourself, you can be a master carpenter.

97
00:09:40,040 --> 00:09:41,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody can be a master carpenter.

98
00:09:41,901 --> 00:09:49,204
[SPEAKER_01]: That is, the nature of human situation allows mastery of carpentry for anyone.

99
00:09:49,885 --> 00:09:51,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I'm a master carpenter.

100
00:09:53,146 --> 00:09:53,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Already.

101
00:09:54,327 --> 00:09:56,008
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you can be.

102
00:09:56,168 --> 00:09:57,088
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean you are.

103
00:09:58,269 --> 00:09:59,810
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first, you have to admit that you're not.

104
00:10:00,910 --> 00:10:03,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Take the ownership of your non-mastery.

105
00:10:04,357 --> 00:10:04,958
[SPEAKER_01]: of carpentry.

106
00:10:05,158 --> 00:10:10,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, in the more pertinent sense, take the ownership of your possessions.

107
00:10:11,424 --> 00:10:13,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you do assume them and that is a starting point.

108
00:10:14,367 --> 00:10:17,710
[SPEAKER_01]: If you train rightly, you will relinquish it all and abandon it.

109
00:10:17,830 --> 00:10:25,238
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the statement of freed from everything, free from the action, you can't own the feelings, all of that will be true, will be applicable to you.

110
00:10:28,442 --> 00:10:32,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's funny, if you start off with nothing is mine.

111
00:10:33,503 --> 00:10:34,363
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all not mine.

112
00:10:34,503 --> 00:10:37,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Even my actions, it's all just the body acting and choices being made.

113
00:10:37,864 --> 00:10:38,864
[SPEAKER_00]: It's got nothing to do with me.

114
00:10:39,384 --> 00:10:40,704
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not responsible for anything.

115
00:10:41,125 --> 00:10:42,185
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the result of that?

116
00:10:43,365 --> 00:10:44,925
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not responsible for anything.

117
00:10:45,365 --> 00:10:48,566
[SPEAKER_00]: The person is just going to do whatever.

118
00:10:48,826 --> 00:10:52,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's basically, that shows the limit, as I said, the limit of logic.

119
00:10:54,684 --> 00:11:01,170
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a true statement, in an impersonal public logic sense, that you are not the owner of your action.

120
00:11:01,650 --> 00:11:03,332
[SPEAKER_01]: But, that is not who you are.

121
00:11:03,372 --> 00:11:05,013
[SPEAKER_01]: That is not where your starting point is.

122
00:11:05,094 --> 00:11:08,877
[SPEAKER_01]: You always... your starting point is your experience, here and now, your point of view.

123
00:11:09,378 --> 00:11:15,323
[SPEAKER_01]: The public view, the public logic, the applicable theory, is secondary to your experience as such.

124
00:11:16,465 --> 00:11:18,927
[SPEAKER_01]: But people put that first, and their own experience second.

125
00:11:19,995 --> 00:11:25,896
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you need to be sort of gripped with a desire to be free from suffering, because that is always personal.

126
00:11:26,276 --> 00:11:28,376
[SPEAKER_01]: That can never be public information.

127
00:11:28,396 --> 00:11:29,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how much people try to make it.

128
00:11:30,377 --> 00:11:32,937
[SPEAKER_01]: When suffering touches you, that's always felt personally.

129
00:11:34,758 --> 00:11:48,380
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you just know this is where it needs to be dealt with, then all this information of aggregates are not yours, and we are already in nibbāna, and all of that, you realize, well, that is a true... that is true only in a certain sense.

130
00:11:49,527 --> 00:11:52,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is a difference between the same information, by the way.

131
00:11:53,129 --> 00:11:59,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, you hear the information, that aggregates are not yours, but you have not been taming your behavior by body, speech and mind.

132
00:12:00,253 --> 00:12:02,695
[SPEAKER_01]: You did not make the effort, you did not develop right endurance.

133
00:12:04,556 --> 00:12:05,476
[SPEAKER_01]: And you get the information.

134
00:12:06,076 --> 00:12:09,298
[SPEAKER_01]: It will only have a certain depth, that information.

135
00:12:09,338 --> 00:12:16,863
[SPEAKER_01]: It can maybe inspire you, give you an excuse, make you feel light, and you don't own anything, you relinquish everything, but fundamentally,

136
00:12:18,018 --> 00:12:24,601
[SPEAKER_01]: You are bound with your ownership, not through information or lack of it, you're bound with your ownership through your actions.

137
00:12:25,241 --> 00:12:27,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Actions infused with greed, aversion, delusion.

138
00:12:27,222 --> 00:12:30,963
[SPEAKER_01]: That is what binds you to owning things and being controlled by them.

139
00:12:32,164 --> 00:12:43,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, imagine somebody who has tamed their behavior by body, speech and mind, has not been giving in to the unwholesome intentions of greed, aversion, delusion, has been taming them, has been enduring them rightly.

140
00:12:44,809 --> 00:12:46,870
[SPEAKER_01]: He hears the information, the same information.

141
00:12:47,982 --> 00:12:52,485
[SPEAKER_01]: The same, same sentence, same logic, same conclusion, same rationale.

142
00:12:53,006 --> 00:12:54,407
[SPEAKER_01]: It will land a lot deeper.

143
00:12:56,869 --> 00:13:05,475
[SPEAKER_01]: And the depth of that is something that the first person can never understand unless he develops his behavior by body speech, unless he goes through the training.

144
00:13:07,517 --> 00:13:11,440
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what it's meant when it says that dhamma is beyond mere reasoning.

145
00:13:12,389 --> 00:13:17,752
[SPEAKER_01]: See, you could reason that, yes, we are not the owners, everything makes sense, and look, everything is changing, and all of that.

146
00:13:18,672 --> 00:13:22,234
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not the dhamma, although it sounds like the dhamma, although it's from the suttas.

147
00:13:23,395 --> 00:13:25,576
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the dhamma, because it lacks that depth.

148
00:13:25,956 --> 00:13:27,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Dhamma begins only after a certain depth.

149
00:13:28,778 --> 00:13:39,824
[SPEAKER_01]: And no amount of your logic and reasoning can show you that depth before you commit to the practice of purification with body, speech, and mind, and arrive at that depth yourself.

150
00:13:43,503 --> 00:13:44,943
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the difference in the nutshell.

151
00:13:49,405 --> 00:13:57,207
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when said that dhamma is beyond reason, does not mean that dhamma is a mystical thing that sort of cannot be thought of and stuff like that.

152
00:13:57,907 --> 00:13:58,048
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

153
00:13:58,108 --> 00:13:59,948
[SPEAKER_01]: It means reason, it's not enough.

154
00:14:01,909 --> 00:14:03,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the Buddha never dismissed the reason.

155
00:14:03,489 --> 00:14:04,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Quite the opposite.

156
00:14:04,229 --> 00:14:10,952
[SPEAKER_01]: He goes and he says, a wise man wishing for his own welfare, he goes and listens to this teacher or that teacher.

157
00:14:10,992 --> 00:14:11,612
[SPEAKER_01]: He ponders.

158
00:14:11,712 --> 00:14:12,212
[SPEAKER_01]: He reasons.

159
00:14:14,263 --> 00:14:26,234
[SPEAKER_01]: He tries to intellectually grasp what is being said and whether it has potential to lead to freedom from suffering or not, whether it's blameworthy, whether it encourages actions that are blameworthy or not, and all of that.

160
00:14:27,155 --> 00:14:31,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Not how he abandons the reason and he focuses on the moment and he has astral projection experience or something.

161
00:14:32,700 --> 00:14:32,820
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

162
00:14:32,900 --> 00:14:33,421
[SPEAKER_01]: He reasons.

163
00:14:34,462 --> 00:14:37,765
[SPEAKER_01]: But once he has reasoned, he also starts doing the other work.

164
00:14:39,470 --> 00:14:49,016
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason that he... the reasoning that he had in the beginning will then grow through his practice of purifying his actions by body, by speech, by mind.

165
00:14:50,376 --> 00:14:57,521
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, eventually, when he arrives at the deeper understanding of the nature of things, sees for himself, he will see, oh, yeah, reason was part of it.

166
00:14:57,621 --> 00:15:01,343
[SPEAKER_01]: If I did not intellectually grasp what needs to be done, I would not have done it.

167
00:15:02,299 --> 00:15:15,447
[SPEAKER_01]: And no amount of revolving around logic would magically take you to that depth, because that depth is only attainable through your own development of purification by body, speech and mind.

168
00:15:15,468 --> 00:15:18,590
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be able to see it yourself, not just hear about it.

169
00:15:18,610 --> 00:15:24,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the reason will then basically, as I said, acquire another dimension.

170
00:15:24,673 --> 00:15:25,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's put it that way.

171
00:15:27,075 --> 00:15:31,638
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, the reasoning is two-dimensional, but you're not aware of it, because you are within those two dimensions.

172
00:15:34,032 --> 00:15:42,077
[SPEAKER_01]: But with the depth that's developed through cultivation of virtue, gradual training, there is a third dimension being developed.

173
00:15:42,617 --> 00:15:44,819
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to the same information, same reason.

174
00:15:46,740 --> 00:15:48,201
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't acquire new reasoning.

175
00:15:48,761 --> 00:15:56,286
